Green coffee beans taste grassy and raw. Roasting is what turns them into coffee, and how far a roaster takes them changes everything. The [origin](/coffee/guides/coffee-origins/), [variety](/coffee/guides/coffee-varieties/) and [processing](/coffee/guides/coffee-processing-methods/) set up the flavors. The roast decides how many of them make it to your cup.

This is part four of [Understanding Coffee Beans](/coffee/guides/understanding-coffee-beans/).

## Light, medium and dark

<CoffeeRoastChart />

- **Light roasts** keep the most of the coffee's own character: the fruit and florals from the variety and the process. They also have the most acidity. This is where most specialty roasters live, especially for pour over.
- **Medium roasts** trade some of that brightness for sweetness and body. Think caramel, nuts and milk chocolate. This is the easiest place to start if light roasts taste sour to you.
- **Dark roasts** taste more like the roast than the bean: smoky, bittersweet and toasty, with oil on the surface. They hold up well to milk, and they're forgiving to brew. The trade-off is that a Kenyan and a Brazilian taste pretty similar at a dark roast.

## Reading roast on a bag

There's no standard for roast names, so one roaster's "medium" can be another's "light." Some roasters skip the words and use a scale or a row of beans instead. You'll also see:

- **Filter roast:** Usually lighter, made for pour over and drip.
- **Espresso roast:** Usually a bit darker, so it's sweeter and easier to pull as a shot.
- **Omni roast:** Meant to work for both.

Names like "French roast" and "Italian roast" are old shorthand for very dark roasts.

## Does dark roast have more caffeine?

Not really. By weight, light and dark roasts have about the same caffeine. Dark roast beans are puffier and lighter, though, so if you measure with a scoop instead of a scale, a scoop of light roast holds a little more coffee and a little more caffeine. It's one more reason to [weigh your beans](/coffee/reviews/hand-coffee-grinders/).

## Check the roast date

A good bag shows a **roast date**, not just a "best by" date. Coffee is at its best for a few weeks after roasting, and fresher isn't always better: freshly roasted beans give off a lot of CO₂ for the first several days, which can make the brew uneven. For pour over and other filter coffee, beans are usually great from about a few days to two weeks after roasting. Espresso and light roasts tend to like a little longer rest. After a month or so, most coffee starts to taste flat.

If a bag doesn't have a roast date at all, I'd skip it.

## Where to start

If you drink your coffee black and want to taste the beans, go light or medium. If you take milk, or you just like a bold, toasty cup, medium-dark and dark roasts are made for that. Either way, buy a bag with a recent roast date and [grind it fresh](/coffee/reviews/hand-coffee-grinders/).

That's the last of the four. To put them all together on a real bag, head back to the [overview](/coffee/guides/understanding-coffee-beans/).
